      <div class="col">
        <!-- the result first, then the evidence it was drawn from -->
        <section class="panel" id="loop-scoreboard" hidden>
          <div class="panel-head"><h2>Benchmark result</h2><span class="label" id="loop-score-sub"></span></div>
          <div class="loop-verdict" id="loop-verdict"></div>
          <ul class="summary-lines" id="loop-score-lines"></ul>
          <p class="caption" id="loop-score-note"></p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="loop-eeg-panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <h2>The recordings <span class="badge math" title={math}>math</span></h2>
            <span class="head-right">
              <span class="label">19-channel scalp EEG</span>
              <label class="opt"><input type="checkbox" id="eeg-all-channels" /> all 19 channels</label>
              <label class="opt"><input type="checkbox" id="eeg-shared-scale" /> one scale for all states</label>
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">{BENCH.reading}</p>
          <ol class="eeg-strips" id="loop-eegs"></ol>
          <p class="caption dim" id="loop-eegs-empty">Nothing has run yet. Each state's recording appears here as the test computes it.</p>
          <details class="howto">
            <summary>how to read a recording</summary>
            <p class="caption"><b>What this is.</b> A <b>scalp EEG</b>: the voltage at each electrode plotted against time, one row per channel, stacked front to back across the head. Every strip here is simulated — a whole-brain run projected onto 19 electrode positions of the international <b>10–20 system</b> — but it is drawn and analysed the way a clinical recording is, because that is what the test turns on: whether a model trained on real human EEG can separate states generated by equations.</p>
            <dl class="keyed">
              <dt>the rows</dt><dd>One per channel, named at the left in 10–20 notation. Six evenly spaced channels by default; tick <b>all 19 channels</b> for the full <b>montage</b>.</dd>
              <dt>± µV</dt><dd>The vertical scale, printed on every strip in microvolts. Each recording is drawn to its own scale so its rhythm stays legible; tick <b>one scale for all states</b> to compare <b>amplitude</b> between states instead.</dd>
              <dt>the gridlines</dt><dd>One second apart, labelled every other second along the bottom.</dd>
              <dt>δ θ α β γ</dt><dd>The <b>band power</b> of that same recording: the share of its power in each conventional band — delta 1–4 Hz, theta 4–8, alpha 8–13, beta 13–30, gamma 30–80.</dd>
              <dt>the two readings</dt><dd>LaBraM's nearest-centroid pick with its confidence, and the blind judge's pick with its own stated certainty. Each strip reports what was said about it; whether it was right is the benchmark result above.</dd>
              <dt>what it is not</dt><dd>A simulated recording, not a patient's. The forward projection is a distance-weighted kernel rather than a boundary-element head model, so electrode positions are approximate and only cortex contributes.</dd>
            </dl>
          </details>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tabpanel" data-panel="glossary" hidden>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="col">
        <section class="panel glossary" id="glossary">
          <div class="panel-head"><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="label">the words this studio uses</span></div>
          <dl>
            {GLOSSARY.map(([term, def]) => (<><dt>{term}</dt><dd>{def}</dd></>))}
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
      <div class="col">
        <section class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head"><h2>What is AI here, and what is not</h2><span class="label">the line, kept in one place</span></div>
          <p class="caption">The studio keeps a hard line between the equations that generate the dynamics and the learned models that read them. Nothing on the left was trained on anything; nothing on the right computes a trajectory. That separation is what makes a result here possible to disagree with.</p>
          <dl class="keyed">
            <dt><span class="badge math">math</span></dt><dd><b>The simulations.</b> The whole-brain model, the cell-by-cell model and the single neuron are systems of equations solved step by step on this machine. Same settings, same numbers, every time.</dd>
            <dt><span class="badge math">math</span></dt><dd><b>The treatments.</b> Each compound is a set of multipliers on those equations’ parameters, written by hand from what the drug does at its receptor.</dd>
            <dt><span class="badge math">math</span></dt><dd><b>The analysis.</b> Mean field, synchrony, band power, latencies and the EEG forward projection are classical signal processing. No model is involved in any of them.</dd>
            <dt><span class="badge ai">AI</span></dt><dd><b>Cajal.</b> A language model that decides which tools to call and explains what they returned. It may not invent a number.</dd>
            <dt><span class="badge ai">AI</span></dt><dd><b>LaBraM.</b> A network trained on real human EEG. It reads four seconds of all nineteen electrodes at once and returns a few hundred numbers describing that stretch. It names nothing: the benchmark tests only whether states that differ produce numbers that differ.</dd>
            <dt><span class="badge ai">AI</span></dt><dd><b>The benchmark’s definer and judge.</b> Language models that design the four test states and, blind, name them back from an analysis.</dd>
            <dt><span class="badge ai">AI</span></dt><dd><b>The other pre-trained models.</b> SwiFT and BrainGFM (fMRI), Geneformer and scGPT (single-cell), Cellpose-SAM (microscopy), each run when you hand Cajal a file. Any that is not configured falls back to classical analysis, and the result says which one ran.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section class="panel spec">
          <div class="panel-head"><h2>Technical specification</h2></div>
          <dl class="keyed">
            <dt>disclaimer</dt><dd>cajal is a simulation studio and a testing harness for algorithms. It is not a clinical or diagnostic instrument, not certified as software in a medical device, and nothing in it should be read as advice about a person.</dd>
            <dt>macroscale</dt><dd>Wilson–Cowan neural-mass formulation over 85 regions: 68 Desikan–Killiany cortical parcels plus 17 deeper nodes — subcortical nuclei, cerebellum and brainstem — on a <b>synthetic</b> distance-rule connectome with conduction delays. The wiring is generated, not measured tractography.</dd>
            <dt>the solver</dt><dd>Plain TypeScript with no dependencies, integrated on the CPU. One copy of it serves both the agent’s tools and this page, so a run makes the same numbers wherever it was started.</dd>
            <dt>geometry</dt><dd>FreeSurfer fsaverage cortex; cerebellum and brainstem from the MNI152 template; sections from the MNI152 T1.</dd>
            <dt>microscale</dt><dd>Sparse random networks of Izhikevich spiking neurons.</dd>
            <dt>single neuron</dt><dd>Nernst and Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equations; Hodgkin–Huxley membrane with Q10 temperature scaling.</dd>
            <dt>AI stack</dt><dd>EEG: LaBraM (active). fMRI: SwiFT, BrainGFM. Single-cell: Geneformer, scGPT. Imaging: Cellpose-SAM. All of them run locally on PyTorch. Agent: Cajal on the Melchizedek runtime (Gemini, or a local Qwen through Ollama).</dd>
            <dt>architecture</dt><dd>Four local services: <span class="mono">:8945</span> Python inference (<span class="mono">npm run models</span>); <span class="mono">:8935 / :8975</span> the simulation tools and this page's event-stream relay (<span class="mono">npm run serve</span>); <span class="mono">:4010</span> the Melchizedek agent service (<span class="mono">npm run a2a</span>); and the page itself (<span class="mono">npm run dev</span>). All four bind to loopback only.</dd>
            <dt>where your data goes</dt><dd><b>Every simulation runs on this machine</b>, as does LaBraM and every other pre-trained model: no recording, image or dataset you open is uploaded. The conversational agent is the exception. By default Cajal is served by Gemini, so the text of your questions and the tool results it reads are sent to that API. Point it at the local Qwen build (<span class="mono">cajal_local</span>) and nothing leaves the machine at all.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

